   "source": [
    "**Insight — D1 Numerical × Numerical Dynamics:**\n",
    "1. **Strong Positive Correlation:** Overtime hours and overtime compensation exhibit an exceptionally strong linear relationship (**Pearson $r = 0.9133$** across all records; **$r = 0.8631$** among positive recipients) and monotonic rank association (**Spearman $\\rho = 0.9579$**).\n",
    "2. **Implied Hourly Wage Rate Dispersion:** Rather than a single fixed rate, the scatter plot displays a classic fan-like expansion. The implied hourly rate ($\text{Total OT Paid} / \text{OT Hours}$) exhibits substantial dispersion: the median implied rate is **$46.16/hour**, but ranges from **$20.84/hr (5th percentile)** to **$98.35/hr (95th percentile)**. This dispersion reflects the fact that overtime premiums are calculated from base hourly rates, which differ substantially by civil service seniority and rank.\n",
    "3. **Non-Causal Interpretation:** While hours worked structurally dictate overtime earnings, this relationship represents an accounting linkage, not independent behavioral causality.\n"
   ]
