   "source": [
    "**Insight — E4 Overtime Reliance & Denominator Mechanics:**\n",
    "1. **Independent Denominator Handling:** Out of 1,846,719 positive overtime records with positive regular pay, exactly **368 records** exhibit invalid Total Gross Compensation values ($\\le 0$, resulting from large negative offsets in `Total Other Pay`). These 368 records were excluded strictly from Metric 1, yielding **1,846,351 valid records** with 100% finite ratios. For Metric 2, because Regular Gross Paid is strictly positive, the ratio was calculated independently across all **1,846,719 records**.\n",
    "2. **Metric 1 Findings (OT / Total Gross Comp):** Among valid records, overtime constitutes a median of **11.46%** (mean: **13.09%**, std: **11.78%**, IQR: 3.46% to 20.50%) of total gross compensation.\n",
    "3. **Metric 2 Findings (OT / Regular Gross Paid):** Relative to base pay, the median reliance ratio is **14.46%** (IQR: 3.89% to 29.41%). The arithmetic mean (**22.51%**, std: 1097.08%) is inflated by extreme valid ratios (up to 978,700%) caused by tiny base pay denominators in partial-year or mid-year appointment records.\n",
    "4. **Severe Reliance Cohort:** Exactly **124,113 payroll records (6.72%)** earn overtime exceeding 50% of regular base salary, and **9,367 payroll records (0.51%)** earn overtime exceeding 100% of regular base pay.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "760faaf0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### E-Final: Multivariate Segment Summary\n",
    "The multivariate analyses establish that municipal overtime expenditure is structured by three interacting forces:\n",
    "1. **Institutional Concentration:** Uniformed emergency agencies account for 81.72% of citywide overtime, driven by round-the-clock staffing mandates and high employee participation (>81%).\n",
    "2. **Wage-Rate Multipliers:** Higher base-salary tiers experience steeper overtime earnings functions, meaning operational surges among senior uniformed personnel generate disproportionate fiscal expenditure.\n",
    "3. **Contractual Divergence:** Salaried annual schedules govern uniformed emergency overtime, while per diem daily schedules govern NYCHA maintenance labor.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "097fff24",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "# SECTION F — EXECUTIVE SUMMARY AND RECOMMENDATIONS\n",
    "\n",
    "### F1. Executive Summary: Core Empirical Findings\n",
    "1. **Extreme Institutional Concentration:** Municipal overtime is not evenly distributed across the municipal workforce; **81.72% ($20.887 billion)** of all overtime across FY2014–FY2025 is concentrated in just five agencies: the Police Department ($9.517B; 37.23%), Fire Department ($4.935B; 19.31%), Department of Correction ($2.795B; 10.93%), Department of Sanitation ($2.060B; 8.06%), and NYCHA ($1.581B; 6.18%).\n",
    "2. **Severe Recipient Skewness:** Across all 6.78 million records, only **27.30% (1,849,942 records)** received positive overtime compensation. Among positive recipients, payments average **$13,817.78** against a citywide median of $0.00 across all payroll records.\n",
    "3. **Longitudinal Expansion (+99.41%):** Citywide annual overtime expenditures nominally doubled from **$1.534 billion in FY2014 to $3.059 billion in FY2024**, before recording $2.922 billion in FY2025. This growth was driven primarily by an escalating average payment per recipient record (**+79.17%**, rising from $10,838.35 to $19,418.77 in FY2024 and $20,923.10 in FY2025) rather than expanding the proportion of employees working overtime.\n",
    "4. **Divergent Labor Contracts:** Overtime in uniformed emergency services is disbursed almost entirely (>92% to 98%) on annual salaried schedules (`Per Annum`). In contrast, the NYC Housing Authority expends **61.12% ($965.75M)** of its overtime on daily trade labor (`Per Day`).\n",
    "5. **Spatial Concentration:** Department of Correction expends **78.48% ($2,193.28M)** of its overtime recorded under Queens, consistent with the major operational facilities at Rikers Island. Manhattan accounts for $7.659 billion in net overtime outlay with a 14.08% participation rate.\n",
    "6. **High Compensation Reliance:** Across 1,846,719 positive overtime records with positive regular pay, overtime represents a median of **14.46%** of regular gross earnings (and a median of 11.46% of total gross compensation). Furthermore, **124,113 payroll records (6.72%)** reflect overtime exceeding 50% of regular base pay, and **9,367 payroll records (0.51%)** reflect overtime exceeding 100% of base pay.\n",
    "7. **Administrative Data Quality Anomalies:** Auditing identified **3,680 negative overtime records** (-$1.23M), **171,966 records with positive overtime pay but zero recorded hours** ($562.77M), and **506,235 records (7.47%)** lacking work location borough identifiers.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "db931ad4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### F2. Section A5 Analytical Expectation Scorecard\n",
    "\n",
    "| Supporting Dimension | Original Section A5 Expectation | Empirical Evidence from Analysis | Verdict | Analytical Caveats |\n",
    "| :--- | :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n",
    "| **`OT Hours`** | Strong positive linear correlation ($r > 0.85$) with overtime payments | Pearson $r = 0.9133$ (all), $r = 0.8631$ (positive); Spearman $\\rho = 0.9579$ (all), $\\rho = 0.8988$ (positive) | **SUPPORTED** | Fan-shaped scatter dispersion reflects diverse base hourly rates across civil service ranks. |\n",
    "| **`Agency Name`** | Extreme concentration in uniformed emergency response departments | Top 5 agencies account for 81.72% ($20.887B) of all overtime; NYPD alone accounts for 37.23% ($9.517B) | **SUPPORTED** | Driven by 24/7/365 emergency response and minimum staffing mandates rather than civilian inefficiency. |\n",
