   "source": [
    "# Programmatic Verification of Section F Consolidated Conclusion Word Count\n",
    "conclusion_text = \"\"\"This exploratory data analysis of the New York City Citywide Payroll dataset examined 6,775,567 verified, deduplicated payroll records across twelve fiscal years (FY2014 through FY2025), encompassing $25.561 billion in cumulative municipal overtime disbursements. The empirical findings provide a quantitative profile of public-sector overtime allocations, institutional concentration, longitudinal trajectories, and compensation patterns across municipal agencies.\n",
    "\n",
    "First, the payroll universe reveals an extreme institutional concentration of overtime disbursements. Overtime expenditure is not uniformly distributed across municipal payroll records; rather, it is concentrated within five primary agencies—the Police Department (NYPD), Fire Department (FDNY), Department of Correction (DOC), Department of Sanitation (DSNY), and the NYC Housing Authority (NYCHA). Together, these five entities account for 81.72% ($20.887 billion) of all municipal overtime payments across the twelve-year period, with the Police Department alone accounting for 37.23% ($9.517 billion). Furthermore, overtime distribution across payroll records is heavily skewed: across all 6.78 million payroll records, only 27.30% (1,849,942 payroll records) received positive overtime compensation, earning an overall average positive disbursement of $13,817.78 against an overall median of $0.00 across all payroll records.\n",
    "\n",
    "Second, the longitudinal analysis demonstrates consistent upward growth in nominal overtime outlays over the decade. Citywide annual overtime expenditures increased from $1.534 billion in FY2014 to $3.059 billion in FY2024, representing a 99.41% nominal increase, before recording $2.922 billion in FY2025. Concurrently, the average payment among payroll records with positive overtime rose from $10,838.35 in FY2014 to $19,418.77 in FY2024, and reached $20,923.10 in FY2025. Across the individual top agencies, annual overtime payments between FY2014 and FY2024 increased by 118.78% in the Police Department (from $575.90M to $1,259.94M), 104.98% in the Department of Correction (from $137.82M to $282.50M), 94.73% in the NYC Housing Authority (from $106.89M to $208.15M), 75.24% in the Fire Department (from $341.86M to $599.08M), and 31.86% in the Department of Sanitation (from $124.56M to $164.24M).\n",
    "\n",
    "Third, multivariate cross-tabulations document distinct labor remuneration and spatial configurations. In the Police Department, Fire Department, Department of Sanitation, and Department of Correction, overtime is overwhelmingly disbursed under annual salaried schedules (Per Annum), ranging between 92.64% and 98.70% of agency overtime. In contrast, the NYC Housing Authority expended 61.12% ($965.75M) of its overtime under daily pay basis schedules (Per Day). Spatially, recorded work locations indicate notable geographic concentrations: the Department of Correction records 78.48% ($2,193.28M) of its overtime outlay in Queens, while the Police Department records 42.16% ($4,012.66M) in Manhattan and 21.09% ($2,006.79M) in Brooklyn. These geographic distributions are consistent with the presence of major operational facilities and commercial centers, though payroll records reflect administrative location codes rather than operational deployments.\n",
    "\n",
    "Fourth, compensation structure analysis indicates that overtime constitutes a meaningful share of gross remuneration among recipient cohorts. Among 1,846,351 valid payroll records with positive regular pay and positive total gross compensation, overtime represents a median of 11.46% of total gross compensation (mean: 13.09%, standard deviation: 11.78%). Relative to regular gross earnings across all 1,846,719 positive records, the median ratio is 14.46% (mean: 22.51%, elevated due to small base-pay denominators in partial-year or nominal records). While for most records overtime remains a supplementary component, 124,113 payroll records (6.72%) reflect overtime exceeding 50% of regular gross pay, and 9,367 payroll records (0.51%) reflect overtime exceeding 100% of regular gross pay.\n",
    "\n",
    "Finally, payroll auditing identified several data-quality characteristics requiring careful methodological handling. The dataset contains 3,680 negative overtime payment records (representing -$1.23 million), 917 negative overtime hours records, and 171,966 payroll records with positive overtime payments but zero recorded overtime hours. Furthermore, 506,235 payroll records (7.47%) omit work location borough identifiers. These patterns represent administrative data characteristics that warrant further auditing and standardized recording protocols.\n",
    "\n",
    "Based on these empirical observations, municipal oversight bodies would benefit from establishing standardized cross-agency payroll reporting protocols, implementing automated tracking for records exhibiting zero hours alongside positive payments, monitoring high overtime reliance ratios across frontline job titles, and conducting comparative fiscal reviews between overtime reliance and base staffing allocations.\"\"\"\n",
